 "why_high_risk": [
  {
   "text": "Infants are the most sensitive group for lead, and formula is consumed in large daily volumes. Healthy Babies Bright Futures' 2019 exposure model ranked infant formula among the 15 foods behind 55 percent of estimated IQ loss from arsenic and lead in children under two, driven by volume, with lead the main metal for formula.",
   "source": "houlihan2019"
  },
  {
   "text": "Consumer Reports detected lead in 34 of 41 powdered formulas tested in 2025 (1.2 to 4.2 ppb); 18 had lead equal to 50 to 100 percent of the California MADL at the intake CR assumed.",
   "source": "cr2025"
  },
  {
   "text": "Consumer Reports speciated nine formulas with total arsenic of 6.7 ppb or more; seven were over its risk level and two were close. The highest inorganic arsenic was in specialty non-soy powders: 19.7 ppb in EleCare Hypoallergenic and 15.1 ppb in Similac Alimentum.",
   "source": "cr2025"
  },
  {
   "text": "FDA tested 312 U.S. formula samples from 16 brands: lead was detected in 80 percent (median 0.2 ppb as prepared, maximum 1.1 ppb), and arsenic, when speciated, was inorganic. FDA reported overall levels as low and opened follow-up on some samples.",
   "source": "fda2026page"
  },
  {
   "text": "The United States has no action level for metals in infant formula, so there is no federal ceiling to test against today; FDA says it is working to establish one.",
   "source": "fda2026press"
  }
 ],
